@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Moving with systems and input") {
        Move the player using a typed ``Component`` and ``System``, establish an explicit system schedule, and reverse direction with an entity-targeted tap.

        @Image(source: "spriteui-tutorial-hero.svg", alt: "Subtle cyan paths connect geometric nodes on a dark background.")
    }

    @Section(title: "Give motion data a typed system") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Complete <doc:creating-your-first-scene> first. As before, each supplied file stands alone: replace the contents of `ContentView.swift` in the host app with the current stage. Keep `ContentView()` in your app entry point unchanged and use only one stage at a time. The observable scene remains retained in `@State`, and its entity work remains on the main actor.

            A component stores velocity in points per second. A system selects entities using ``EntityQuery`` and advances their positions with the frame's scaled ``UpdateContext/deltaTime``. GameView's renderer drives these frames; the SwiftUI update closure does not serve as the animation clock.

            The `@MainActor` system converts the context's elapsed seconds to Float, multiplies velocity by elapsed time, and updates position. This first version clamps x at 100 so the player stays visible. The scene installs the system in its own world with the throwing `systems.add` API and reports a scheduling failure in the interface.

            @Image(source: "spriteui-systems-input.svg", alt: "A moving player carries velocity data and responds to a tap.")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Replace the contents of `ContentView.swift` with this complete stage. Define the value component `MovementMotion`, query for entities that have it, and read each entity's component using its concrete Swift type.

                Run the app. The player starts at x = -100, travels right at 80 points per second, and stops at x = 100. It should reach the limit in about 2.5 simulation seconds.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-movement-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-movement-01-preview.svg", alt: "Diagram: Velocity advances each frame.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Order movement before boundary response") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            ``SystemDependency`` orders installed systems. A dependency does not install another system for you. Both systems here use the default simulation phase; the bounds system declares that it runs after the movement system. All simulation systems precede the presentation phase.

            The resulting schedule runs movement and then bounds each frame, regardless of insertion order. Invalid cycles or phase constraints throw instead of silently replacing the schedule.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Replace the contents of `ContentView.swift` with this stage. Remove the clamp from translation and introduce a bounds system. It clamps overshoot at either x limit, reverses the velocity, and stores the changed component back on the entity. Install bounds before movement to show that the dependency determines execution order. Both installations remain explicit.

                Run the app. The player patrols continuously between x = -100 and x = 100. This is a game rule for a patrol area; the next tutorial adds geometric wall collision.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-movement-02.swift", previousFile: "spriteui-movement-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-movement-02-preview.svg", alt: "Diagram: Ordered systems keep the player between limits.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Target input and bridge pause state") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            ``InputTargetComponent`` opts the player into targeted input. An explicit ``CollisionComponent`` supplies a 44-point interaction rectangle around its 36-point sprite. The gesture resolves the nearest matching ancestor with the motion component and delivers its captured entity to a main-actor callback.

            The SwiftUI update closure reads observable `isMoving` and enables or disables only the movement system. Bounds still runs, and buttons and targeted taps remain usable. This is a movement toggle; a full simulation pause uses the world's separate pause setting.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Replace the contents of `ContentView.swift` with this stage. Attach `SpatialTapGesture().targetedToEntity(where:)` directly to the single GameView and add `onEnded` after targeting. Read and write the captured entity's motion component in that callback. Don't save the gesture value or use a global last-touched entity. Run the app, tap or click the cyan player, and try Pause movement and Resume movement.

                Tapping the player reverses it and increases the tap count; tapping empty scene space does neither. The explicit collider defines the slightly larger hit area. Targeted input uses displayed transforms, including any camera or presentation animation. Pause movement stops translation; Resume movement restores it. Continue with <doc:steering-and-collision-aware-movement>.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-movement-03.swift", previousFile: "spriteui-movement-02.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-movement-03-preview.svg", alt: "Diagram: Tap the player to reverse; pause stops motion.")
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
